---
export const title = 'Editing';
export const order = 400;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  <code>editable</code> lets people drag an event to move it and drag its top or bottom edge to change when it starts
  or ends, in steps of <code>minute-step</code> minutes (15 by default). All-day events, and every event in the month
  view, move and resize by whole days. Each drop fires the cancelable <code>event-change</code> with
  <code>&#123; event, start, end, action &#125;</code>; unless you cancel it, the calendar replaces <code>events</code> with a
  new array holding a copy of the event with its new times. To save a change on the server first, cancel the event and
  apply it later with <code>applyCalendarChange(events, detail)</code>. Give an event <code>editable: false</code> to
  keep it in place; <code>events-readonly</code> turns editing off.
</p>

<p>
  <code>addable</code> lets people drag across empty time, or click it, to add an event. <code>event-add</code> fires
  with <code>&#123; start, end, allDay &#125;</code>: a click gives <code>default-duration</code> minutes (60 by default), and the
  all-day row and the month view give whole days. The calendar does not add the event itself: it marks the range as a
  new event until <code>events</code> changes, so you can ask for a title first. Call
  <code>clearPlaceholder()</code> if that is canceled. <code>end</code> is exclusive throughout: an all-day event on
  November 4 ends at midnight on November 5.
</p>

<p>
  On touch screens, people hold an event briefly before dragging it, so a swipe still scrolls. Escape cancels a drag,
  and each change is announced to screen readers. Dragging needs a pointer, so also let people change an event's time
  another way, such as in the dialog you open on <code>event-click</code>.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <wc-calendar id='editing-calendar' view='week' editable addable style='height: 700px; width: 100%'></wc-calendar>
  <script is:inline>
    (function() {
      const $calendar = document.getElementById('editing-calendar');

      const today = new Date();
      today.setHours(0, 0, 0, 0);

      function at(days, hours, minutes) {
        const result = new Date(today);
        result.setDate(result.getDate() + days);
        result.setHours(hours, minutes || 0, 0, 0);
        return result;
      }

      let nextId = 1;
      $calendar.events = [
        { id: nextId++, title: 'Standup', start: at(0, 9), end: at(0, 9, 30) },
        { id: nextId++, title: 'Design review', start: at(1, 11), end: at(1, 12), color: 'red' },
        { id: nextId++, title: 'Focus time', start: at(-1, 14), end: at(-1, 16), color: 'blue' },
        { id: nextId++, title: 'Offsite', start: at(2, 0), end: at(4, 0), color: 'orange' },
        { id: nextId++, title: 'Public holiday', start: at(-2, 0), end: at(-1, 0), color: 'green', editable: false },
      ];

      // The calendar applies moves and resizes itself; cancel the event to keep one.
      $calendar.addEventListener('event-change', function(evt) {
        console.log('Changed:', evt.detail.action, evt.detail.event.title, evt.detail.start, evt.detail.end);
      });

      // The calendar only marks the range; adding the event keeps it.
      $calendar.addEventListener('event-add', function(evt) {
        $calendar.events = [...$calendar.events, { id: nextId++, title: 'New event', ...evt.detail }];
      });
    })();
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>
